# Ataxia - Literature Search on UpToDate <span class="tag" tag-name="Reference"><span class="smallcaps">Reference</span></span>

- **References:**
  - [UpToDate: Overview of cerebellar ataxia in adults](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/overview-of-cerebellar-ataxia-in-adults?search=ataxia&source=search_result&selectedTitle=1~150&usage_type=default&display_rank=1)

## Overview of cerebellar ataxia in adults

- **Midline cerebellar dysfunction** - dysfunction of the vermis, the globus and emboliform nuclei, the vestibulocerebellum (flocullonodular lobe), and the intermediate zone:
  - <u>Imbalance</u> - sensation of disequilibirum when standing w/ feet together (i.e. +ve Romberg test), compenated by a wide-based gait
  - <u>Truncal ataxia</u> - characterised by swaying of the head and trunk when sitting, subsequently inability to sit unsupported by the arms
  - <u>Titubation</u> - involuntary, semirhythmic nodding of the head, neck and trunk
  - <u>Dysmetria</u> - LL dysmetria typically more prominent than UL dysmetria (as elicited by heel-to-shin test)
  - <u>Nystagmus</u> - gaze-evoked nystagmus in all directions are often seen
  - <u>Saccadic intrusions</u> - irregular bursts of rapid eye movements that include opsoclonus, occular flutter, square wave jerks, and macrosaccadic oscillations
  - <u>Vertigo</u> - hallucination of movement despite standing still, localises to the vestibulocerebellum
- **Hemispheric cerebellar dysfunction** - dysfunction of the dentate nucleus on one side:
  - <u>Dysdiadochokinesis</u> - incordination when performing rapidly lternative movements
  - <u>Dysmetria</u> - inability to perform accurate movements in goal-directed task due to faulty estimation of distance, where hemispheric cerebellar dysfunction results in both UL (past-pointing) and LL (heel-to-shin test) dysmetria
  - <u>Intention tremor</u> - kinetic tremor characterised by an increased eamplitude as the hand moves closer to its target
  - <u>Ataxic dysarthria</u> (scanning speech) - alternative loudness and fluctuating pitch levels, with emphasis placed on syllables that should not be stressed, w/ or w/o pause in between syllables and words
- **Non-cerebellar causes of ataxia:**
  - Weakness - weakness in muscles may mimick clinical pictures similar to ataxia and must be r/o
  - Sensory loss (proprioceptive and visuals)

# Hypertension, Hypertensive Urgency, and Hypertensive Emergency - Literature Search on UpToDate <span class="tag" tag-name="Reference"><span class="smallcaps">Reference</span></span>

- **References:**
  - [UpToDate: Evaluation and treatment of hypertensive emergencies in adults](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/evaluation-and-treatment-of-hypertensive-emergencies-in-adults?search=malignant%20hypertension&source=search_result&selectedTitle=1~58&usage_type=default&display_rank=1)
  - [UpToDate: Drugs used for the treatment of hypertensive emergencies](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/drugs-used-for-the-treatment-of-hypertensive-emergencies?search=malignant+hypertension&topicRef=3837&source=see_link)
  - [UpToDate: Management of severe asymptomatic hypertension (hypertensive urgencies) in adults](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/management-of-severe-asymptomatic-hypertension-hypertensive-urgencies-in-adults?search=malignant+hypertension&topicRef=3837&source=see_link)

## Evaluation and treatment of hypertensive emergencies in adults

- **Definition and terminology:**
  - <u>Hypertensive urgency</u> - significantly elevated BP (\>= 180/120 mmHg) in the absence of S/S reflecting acute TOD
  - <u>Hypertensive emergency</u> - significantly elevated BP (\>= 180/120 mmHg) in the presence of S/S of acute, ongoing TOD
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - **HPI** - note any ongoing pregnancy, previous TBI (raised ICP), or use of illicit drugs
  - **Associated Sx** - evidence of TOD:
    - **Neurological TOD:**
      - <u>Focal neurological S/S</u> - suggests complicated by an ischaemic or haemorrhagic stroke
      - <u>Generalised neurological S/S</u> (e.g. delirium, stupor, seizures) - may be caused by various factors, e.g. hypertensive encephalopathy, raised ICP, eclampsia in a pregnant women
      - <u>Blurred vision</u> - may reflect a focal posterior-circulation problem, or central retinal vein occlusion (if affectiing one eye)
      - <u>N/V</u> - marked N/V which may relieve headache is suggestive of state of raised ICP
    - **Cardiovascular TOD:**
      - <u>SOB</u> - acute dyspnoea may be a result of acute decompensated heart failure resulting in pulmonary oedema
      - <u>Chest pain</u> - may be due to myocardial ischaemia or aortic dissection
      - <u>Back pain</u> - acute severe back pain may reflect aortic dissection
    - **Renal TOD:**
      - <u>Urine output</u> - reduced urine output suggestive of AKI
      - <u>Haematuria</u> - previous haematuria may reflect a <u>cause of the HTN</u>
  - **PMH:**
    - <u>Known HTN</u> - note that hypertensive emergencies may occur in the <u>absence of known HTN</u> (e.g. drug-induced, good past health, pre-eclampsia)
    - <u>Known vascular comorbidities</u> (AAA) - at high risk of associated complications
  - **Drug Hx:**
    - <u>Use of illicit drugs</u> - e.g. amphetamines, cocaine
    - <u>Prescription drugs</u> - monoamine oxidase inhibitor, or recent discontinuation of clonidine, recent discontinuation of antihypertensive agents (rarely)
- **P/E:**
  - **BP measurement** - persistently severe elevation of BP (\>= 180/120 mmHg)
  - **Fundoscopy** - non-invasive assessment of TOD (grade III+ hypertensive retinopathy necessary for Dx of hypertensive emergency):
    - <u>Grade I</u> - silver wiring
    - <u>Grade II</u> - AV nipping
    - <u>Grade III</u> - flame-shaped haemorrhages, cotton wool spots
    - <u>Grade IV</u> - papilloedema (consider raised ICP w/ appropriate clinical context)
  - **Systems review:**
    - <u>Neurological examination</u> - focal neurological S/S, assessment of GC
    - <u>Cardiovascular examination</u> - any comorbid cardiovascular disease (e.g. detection of cardiomegaly)
    - <u>Chest examination</u> - basal crepitations
- **Ix** - evaluation of TOD and potential secondary cause of HTN:
  - **CXR** - provision of triple-rule out:
    - <u>Findings of acute pulmonary oedema</u> (ADHF) - upper lobe cephalisation, Kerley B lines, alveolar oedema, bat wing appearance
    - <u>Findings of acute aortic dissection</u>:
      - Mediastinal widening
      - Left-sided pulmonary effusion if type A aortic dissection
  - **ECG:**
    - <u>ST/T changes</u> - r/o ongoing mycoardial ischaemia
    - <u>Chamber enlargements</u> - LVH as suggested by Tall R wave in V5/6
  - **Urine** - urine pregnancy test, urine dipstick, urinalysis:
    - <u>Urine pregnancy test</u> - performed in all F of reproductive age
    - <u>Urine dipstick and urinalysis</u> - proteinuria, and haematuria
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC, LRFT, troponin, NT-proBNP:
    - <u>CBC</u> - concomitent clues for underlying pathology
    - <u>LRFT</u> - baseline functions; RFT for contrast studies
    - <u>Troponin</u> - if ACS suspected
    - <u>NT-proBNP</u> - if ADHF suspected
  - **Additional Ix** - however should not delay BP lowering in such patients awaiting these Ix:
    - <u>Neuroimaging</u> (CT/ MRI brain) - if Hx of traumatic brain injury, or +ve neurological S/S
    - <u>Echocardiography</u> - if suspected aortic dissection
    - <u>Contrast CT angiography</u> - if suspected aortic dissection
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Dx and Mx concomittent TOD</u> - e.g. specific Mx such as ACS, ischaemic stroke, intracerebral haemorrhage, and aortic dissection, and also guides BP lowering
    - <u>BP lowering therapy</u> - appropriate target of BP is not evidenced based, but consensus holds that gradual BP lowering over D1, except in certain cases
  - **BP lowering** - <u>gradual BP lowering</u> is generally preferred as to avoid ischaemic damage due to loss of autoregulation in vascular beds habituated in high BP
    - **BP target** - gradual BP reduction by approximately 10-20% in 1H (i.e. \< 180/120 mmHg), and additional 5-15% in subsequent 23H (i.e. \< 160/110 mmHg)
    - **Exceptions to gradual BP lowering:**
      - <u>Acute ischaemic stroke</u> - threshold for BP lowering is raised as it reflects a <u>compensatory increased MAP to maintain cerebral perfusion</u>:
        - In those who are candidates of reperfusion therapy, BP is only lowered if \> 185/110 mmHg
        - In those who are candidates of reperfusion therapy, BP is only lowered if \> 220/110 mmHg
      - <u>Acute aortic dissection</u> - rapid lowering of SBP to 100-120 mmHg to reduce aortic shear force
      - <u>Acute intracerebral haemorrhage</u> - remains controversial and likely agent-specific
    - **RoA** - typically IV anti-hypertensives in the initial 24h, followed by PO

